WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.800
Leon: So, welcome again, Volodya, to the American
Enterprise Institute, [Russian].

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:05.170
Vladimir: It's great to be here.

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:06.920
Thanks very much.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:15.360
Leon: So, I hope to speak today, to cover
a lot of ground today, obviously what Putin's

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:21.390
doing inside Russia and outside Russia.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:32.220
What do you and other pro-democracy activists
are doing in turn to oppose him and try to

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:37.110
build a democratic and free and prosperous
Russia.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:46.120
Secondly, what can the United States do, especially
given that we're in the midst of our primaries?

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:54.440
And of course, we'll talk about Putin's foreign
policy, why he's doing what he's doing, and

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:59.010
where do you think he's going to stop, if
ever.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:08.830
And finally, I hope, given that you're a Cambridge-trained
historian, to talk about the future of Russia

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:13.740
and the future of the regime, something that
you've written about a lot and thought about

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:17.850
over the years and, of course, have the first-hand
knowledge now.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:24.670
I can't think of a single person who could
give us as good and in-depth a picture of

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:25.910
what's happening in Russia as you.

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:28.450
Vladimir: You're too kind, but I'll be happy
to try.

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:30.610
Leon: I'm sure we'll succeed.

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:41.710
So, the first question is a personal one actually,
personal to you, but I think it encapsulates

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:52.780
the nasty or probably bordering the bizarre
atmosphere now inside Russia.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:02:02.330
Last week, the Chechen dictator, maybe soon
a caliph, Ramzan Kadyrov, posted a surveillance-like

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:09.380
video on his Instagram account where you and
another opposition leader Mikhail Kasyanov,

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:15.099
former Prime Minister, looked like being in
crosshairs.

00:02:15.099 --> 00:02:22.170
Now, given that your friend and colleague,
Boris Nemtsov, was killed last year, and given

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:28.959
that you were nearly poisoned to death, or
so it seems, and that seems to be the consensus

00:02:28.959 --> 00:02:36.720
now, that you were deliberately poisoned,
what do you think of this instance of harassment?

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:39.849
But maybe give us a broader sense as well.

00:02:39.849 --> 00:02:45.209
Vladimir: Given Mr. Kadyrov's background and
reputation, both Mikhail Kasyanov and I consider

00:02:45.209 --> 00:02:50.819
this video to be a direct threat, to be direct
and public incitement to murder, and in fact

00:02:50.819 --> 00:02:56.549
we have already submitted an official request
for a criminal probe into this posting, this

00:02:56.549 --> 00:02:57.950
publication.

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:02.079
In fact, in the last few weeks, we have seen
how serious of what seem to be coordinated

00:03:02.079 --> 00:03:07.849
attacks and threats issued by Mr. Kadyrov
himself as well as his close henchmen against

00:03:07.849 --> 00:03:11.219
Kremlin opponents, against Russian opposition
leaders.

00:03:11.219 --> 00:03:15.730
And in fact, just a few days ago, Mr. Kasyanov
was physically attacked in Moscow by people

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:19.669
who looked like Chechens, and in fact there
were three serving members of the Russian

00:03:19.669 --> 00:03:24.250
Interior Ministry in Chechnya who were briefly
detained after this.

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:32.230
And frankly, I think this whole series of
events is a direct result of the brazen impunity

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:37.730
that has surrounded the assassination of our
friend, my friend, Russian opposition leader

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:43.909
Boris Nemtsov, who was gunned down 200 yards
away from the Kremlin nearly a year ago in

00:03:43.909 --> 00:03:45.469
February 2015.

00:03:45.469 --> 00:03:52.090
Despite the fact that traces of this crime
evidently lead to Grozny or at least through

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:53.090
Grozny...

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:54.090
Leon: The capital of Chechnya.

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:55.090
Vladimir: Absolutely.

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:57.049
Or at least through the capital of Chechnya.

00:03:57.049 --> 00:04:03.469
And despite the fact that those arrested on
suspicion of murdering Boris were serving

00:04:03.469 --> 00:04:07.189
members of the Interior Ministry of the Russian
Federation in Chechnya.

00:04:07.189 --> 00:04:08.359
Leon: Victorian Guard, I would think.

00:04:08.359 --> 00:04:09.359
Vladimir: Absolutely.

00:04:09.359 --> 00:04:13.209
Despite the fact that Mr. Kadyrov publicly
called the man accused of killing Boris Nemtsov

00:04:13.209 --> 00:04:19.139
to be called a true patriot, and despite the
numerous requests by the lawyers representing

00:04:19.139 --> 00:04:23.800
Boris Nemtsov's children to question Mr. Kadyrov
regarding this case, he wasn't questioned

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:26.500
even formally by the investigators.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:31.110
We hear no criticism of him or his actions
or his public threats from the Kremlin, in

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:33.320
fact we hear the opposite.

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:38.830
We hear, we continue to hear laudatory statements
from the top Russian leadership with regard

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:39.960
to Mr. Kadyrov.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:45.370
Just a few days ago, Vladimir Putin publicly
thanked Mr. Kadyrov for his effective work,

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:47.030
this is a quote.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:52.690
And Kremlin Chief of Staff Sergei Ivanov said
on record that the Russian leadership has

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:54.419
no questions for Mr. Kadyrov.

00:04:54.419 --> 00:04:58.870
Well, I think after such statements and after
such words, there are no questions left for

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:01.259
the Russian leadership itself.

00:05:01.259 --> 00:05:06.210
But on the substance of your question, yes,
we know that it's a dangerous location to

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:08.039
be in opposition to Vladimir Putin's regime.

00:05:08.039 --> 00:05:12.270
We have seen too many confirmations of that
already.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:15.680
So we know the risks, we understand the risks.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:21.060
But frankly I think, we, those who view the
future of Russia differently from what the

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:29.740
present regime does, we have no right to stand
down or to give up or to run away because

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:32.110
it's our country and we have to fight for
it.

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:38.790
Leon: Well, let's talk about this fight.

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:44.300
Last fall, Russia held local elections.

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:52.180
And your party, the party of People's Freedom,
known also as Parnas, was the only one that

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:58.080
was allowed to participate, and then only
in one out of 85 Russian regions.

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:07.120
I mean, clearly as you gear up, and that's
the essence of your work as I understand it

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:13.699
now, in Russia, as you gear up for the National
Parliament, Duma elections, this September,

00:06:13.699 --> 00:06:21.879
this coming September, given this past record,
and given the record of the past, God knows,

00:06:21.879 --> 00:06:24.620
10, 15 years, where is the hope?

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:30.159
Does it all seem hopeless and yet you continue
your work?

00:06:30.159 --> 00:06:33.099
Vladimir: Well, first of all, let's just get
one thing clear.

00:06:33.099 --> 00:06:37.440
There hasn't been a free and fair election
in Russia in at least 16 years.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.889
If we judge by the statements and evaluations
of international monitors, namely from the

00:06:42.889 --> 00:06:48.600
OSCE and from the Council of Europe, the last
election in Russia that corresponded at least

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:52.689
partially through the European standards of
free and fair elections were held in March

00:06:52.689 --> 00:06:55.770
of 2000, so that's 16 years ago.

00:06:55.770 --> 00:07:00.860
Every election since then has been characterized
by suppression of the opposition or outright

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:02.999
disqualification of the opposition, as you
mentioned.

00:07:02.999 --> 00:07:07.879
And in these regional elections, for instance
last year, the People's Freedom Party was

00:07:07.879 --> 00:07:13.180
prevented from participating in the elections
in Novosibirsk, in Kaluga, in Magadan, initially

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:14.389
in Novgorod.

00:07:14.389 --> 00:07:16.419
And this is the usual picture.

00:07:16.419 --> 00:07:21.710
You have to understand that as a rule, genuine,
real opposition to the Kremlin is not even

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:27.969
allowed to formally participate in the election
process, and we're off the ballot ahead of

00:07:27.969 --> 00:07:28.969
time.

00:07:28.969 --> 00:07:34.110
In those few instances when the opposition
is allowed to be on the ballot, we scored

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:40.199
pretty good results such as when Boris Nemtsov
won his legislative seat in Yaroslavl in 2013

00:07:40.199 --> 00:07:45.310
when Alexei Navalny received, even according
to official results, nearly 30% of the vote

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:48.610
in Moscow in the mayoral election in 2013.

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:54.620
So, basically elections in Putin's Russia
has been turned into a meaningless ritual

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:59.180
to formalize the regime and to continue its
stay in power.

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:05.729
However, we believe, and I believe, and this
is also from historical experience, that boycotts

00:08:05.729 --> 00:08:09.029
and passive posture never work.

00:08:09.029 --> 00:08:16.539
And I think we have to use, even this bogus
and sham and watered-down election process,

00:08:16.539 --> 00:08:22.259
as it exists today, in order to try to get
our message across to as many of our compatriots

00:08:22.259 --> 00:08:29.789
as we can, and also to help young up and coming
political opposition, civil society activists

00:08:29.789 --> 00:08:35.120
to gain some political experience by participating
in these campaigns, to gain some reputation,

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:38.200
to gain name recognition.

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:44.090
And this is precisely what we plan to do this
year, this September, on September 18th, when

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:48.610
Russia will hold the National Parliamentary
elections, or so-called elections, but nevertheless.

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:53.530
So, for instance, the Open Russia Movement,
of which I'm the national coordinator, has

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:55.320
tried twice already last year.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:59.560
First on the municipal level in St. Petersburg,
and then on the regional level in Novosibirsk

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:00.560
to support candidates.

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:06.510
In both times, candidates whom we supported,
even again according to the official results,

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:09.760
came in second place with more than 20% of
the vote.

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:13.320
This is a really far cry from the 1.5% or
2% for the opposition and the Kremlin would

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:14.370
like people to believe.

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:20.750
So this year we're preparing to support 10,
15, maybe 20 candidates for the State Duma

00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:24.370
in single-member districts across Russia,
mostly in big cities of course, but still

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:30.020
across Russia, we're not talking about just
Moscow, in order again to get our message

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:36.010
across, this pro-democracy, pro-European alternative
message to what the Kremlin is offering to

00:09:36.010 --> 00:09:40.580
as many people as we can, and to help our
supporters, young opposition and civil society

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:44.140
activists, to gain this experience.

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:48.190
Because nothing is forever and this regime
is not forever, and there will come a time

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:53.760
when new generation of pro-democracy Russians
will have to take responsibility for the country

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:56.570
and we want to start getting them ready for
that today.

00:09:56.570 --> 00:09:57.570
Leon: Excellent.

00:09:57.570 --> 00:10:01.440
We will talk about Russia's future very soon.

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:06.460
But let me go back to, as announced, to foreign
policy.

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:16.700
Now, the word that's most commonly used, in
fact, we could count the number of times when

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:22.300
this word was used or has been used by the
Obama administration, high officials, state

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:27.440
department, White House, White House spokesman,
in reaction to Putin's foreign policy, and

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:28.690
that word is "surprised."

00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:30.040
They're constantly surprised.

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:31.780
I mean, literally they're surprised.

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:41.070
I could say some of them are saying, I'm quoting
the spokesman for the White House, "Mr. Putin's

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:47.810
intentions are hard to discern," or, this
is my favorite, "We're dying to know what

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:48.900
Mr. Putin is doing."

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:54.950
Are you surprised by what he is doing?

00:10:54.950 --> 00:10:56.880
And if not, why not?

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:02.280
Vladimir: Well, frankly, speaking for me personally,
I understood everything they needed to understand

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:03.280
about Mr. Putin.

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:07.920
And I even remember the date, it was the 20th
of December 1999 when he was still prime minister,

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:10.300
11 days from being acting president.

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:16.370
On that day, this was the day of the Chekist,
the day of the KGB or NKVD.

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:18.660
On that day, he did three things.

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:23.160
He opened a memorial plaque to Yuri Andropov
on Lubyanka Square on the KGB building.

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:24.811
Leon: Right, the former head of the KGB.

00:11:24.811 --> 00:11:29.090
Vladimir: This is, of course, the notorious
head of the KGB who was instrumental in the

00:11:29.090 --> 00:11:34.700
campaign against dissenters, who invented
this punitive psychiatry and actively promoted

00:11:34.700 --> 00:11:36.130
it and so forth.

00:11:36.130 --> 00:11:41.890
On the same day, he spoke at a big gathering
of KGB veterans and he told them that a group

00:11:41.890 --> 00:11:45.320
of KGB employees that has been sent undercover
into the Russian government has fulfilled

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:46.320
their mission.

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:48.740
Everybody thought it was a joke at the time,
but he wasn't joking.

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:54.170
And the third thing he did on the same day
was to raise a glass to Joseph Stalin, because

00:11:54.170 --> 00:11:56.820
this is also Stalin's birthday, the 20th of
December...

00:11:56.820 --> 00:11:57.820
Leon: Ah, that's right, of course.

00:11:57.820 --> 00:12:00.330
Vladimir: ... when he was meeting party leaders
in the Moscow White House.

00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:04.850
So, for me, everything that needed to be understood
about Mr. Putin, I understood on that day.

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:11.440
Other people understood it even much earlier,
so I can't claim to be an oracle or anything

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:12.440
like that on this.

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:18.360
But I think everything he's now doing, in
terms of aggression outside of the Russian

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:23.820
borders, in terms of his foreign policy, is
direct consequences of what he's been doing

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:28.920
domestically since the very first days after
he came to power.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:35.860
There is no reason, in my mind, to expect
a regime that tramples on the rights of its

00:12:35.860 --> 00:12:45.140
own citizens and that disregards its own constitution
to, for some reason, respect the rights of

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:47.790
its neighboring countries and to respect international
law.

00:12:47.790 --> 00:12:48.790
There's no reason to expect that.

00:12:48.790 --> 00:12:51.590
I think one comes directly from another.

00:12:51.590 --> 00:12:56.330
And many of us in the Russian opposition have
said this for a long time.

00:12:56.330 --> 00:13:02.850
Unfortunately, not a lot of people among Western
elites chose to hear it.

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:07.180
And I think frankly if the leaders of Western
democracy has taken a firmer and more principled

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:11.730
stand against the Putin regime earlier on,
we would not be seeing the excesses we're

00:13:11.730 --> 00:13:12.730
seeing today.

00:13:12.730 --> 00:13:16.200
But we are where we are, and better late than
never.

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:22.760
And I hope that at least now the leaders of
the democratic nations of the West know, "Who

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:23.760
is Mr. Putin?"

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:27.200
A famous question that was posed in Davos
16 years ago now.

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:28.370
Just think about that number.

00:13:28.370 --> 00:13:32.820
It's almost a full generation that's passed
in Russia since Mr. Putin came to power.

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:36.750
He's now been in power almost as long as Brezhnev
has, just two years away from that number.

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:44.200
Leon: Yeah, he's about to start competing
with Stalin if you count Stalin's single-man

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:51.360
dictatorship, full dictator, since about 1929
then to '53, that's 24 years.

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:52.500
Vladimir: Yes.

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:54.140
Leon: He's been in a striking distance.

00:13:54.140 --> 00:13:57.550
Vladimir: Well, I'm certain he's not going
to get to that, but it's certainly coming

00:13:57.550 --> 00:13:58.550
close.

00:13:58.550 --> 00:13:59.550
You're right.

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:05.370
Leon: Just to close up on this, Syria, everybody
is concerned about Syria.

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:10.100
Again, every time Russia does something, every
time they bring more troops, every time they

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:16.550
move, they establish yet another base, this
time a stone's throw from Turkish border,

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:19.830
a NATO member, the official Washington is
surprised.

00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:21.260
Surprised, surprised, surprised.

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:23.170
What's the endgame there in Syria?

00:14:23.170 --> 00:14:27.810
Vladimir: Well, I think as with all of the
foreign policy adventures of this current

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:32.740
regime, the origins are domestic and are found
inside.

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:39.710
And once Eastern Ukraine, the Donbass became
a quagmire for the regime, once Ukraine proved

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:47.040
a too difficult target, and the so-called
Novorossiya Project clearly failed, the Kremlin's

00:14:47.040 --> 00:14:53.420
propaganda machine needed new ammunition,
a new fodder to maintain, to revive rather,

00:14:53.420 --> 00:15:01.960
the fate of the strongman image, to show another
round of television pictures purporting to

00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:08.480
be a great power, to be sending troops halfway
across the world in an instant, to be an equal

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:10.270
in the world stage, if you will.

00:15:10.270 --> 00:15:12.790
And this is when Syria came in.

00:15:12.790 --> 00:15:17.010
And if you look at the Kremlin-controlled
television channels in Russia, which is of

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:21.170
course all the major television channels in
Russia today, you will see that Syria has

00:15:21.170 --> 00:15:24.660
replaced Ukraine as the main subject.

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:29.570
Whereas before, they used to show all the
time the Donbass and the fight against the

00:15:29.570 --> 00:15:33.590
so-called fascists and Banderavites in the
Donbass, now they're sharing Syria and the

00:15:33.590 --> 00:15:39.580
so-called fight against terrorism that Putin
is, therefore, conducting in Syria.

00:15:39.580 --> 00:15:44.040
Others, of course, in additional very important
reason, I think that the Kremlin is doing

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:51.790
this, the informal club of dictators is a
pretty loyal one, and Mr. Putin doesn't like

00:15:51.790 --> 00:15:53.870
to see a fellow despot fall from power.

00:15:53.870 --> 00:15:59.170
He's already seen too many in his time, from
Gaddafi to Yanukovych.

00:15:59.170 --> 00:16:04.960
And I think an additional goal in this is
to help preserve Bashar al-Assad in power

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:09.260
in Syria and, of course, it's evident now
to everybody that the chief targets of the

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:14.740
Russian Air Force in Syria are not in fact
ISIS strongholds, but are the anti-Assad opposition

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:15.740
forces.

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:19.940
Leon: Well, back to the United States.

00:16:19.940 --> 00:16:27.840
The presidential primaries are under way,
and unlike Russians, we don't know who will

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:30.180
be the president.

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:36.250
Now, what would be your advice to the presidential
hopefuls?

00:16:36.250 --> 00:16:40.760
Vladimir: Well, I think, first of all, it's
important to state that it is our job and

00:16:40.760 --> 00:16:46.380
our task to bring political changes in Russia,
to bring a democratic future for Russia.

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:50.180
This is a task for us, the Russian opposition,
not for anybody from the outside.

00:16:50.180 --> 00:16:55.690
But I think the best thing the U.S. government
and the future U.S. president, whoever he

00:16:55.690 --> 00:17:03.690
or she might be, can do with regard to Russia
is, frankly, to stand on the position of principle

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:10.860
and to make sure that relationship with the
Kremlin and with Russia is informed, above

00:17:10.860 --> 00:17:15.790
all, by the values, the fundamental values
that form the basis of what the United States

00:17:15.790 --> 00:17:16.790
is all about.

00:17:16.790 --> 00:17:21.870
There's, unfortunately, too often being two
extremes in the way the U.S. government deals

00:17:21.870 --> 00:17:23.819
with Russia and the Kremlin.

00:17:23.819 --> 00:17:29.030
One extreme has been to blame the entire Russian
nation and Russia as a country for the actions

00:17:29.030 --> 00:17:31.000
of the unelected regime that we have now.

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:38.590
And the other extreme is to befriend the Kremlin
dictator and to pretend that Putin is a fully

00:17:38.590 --> 00:17:40.060
fledged friend and partner.

00:17:40.060 --> 00:17:44.930
Unfortunately, we've seen wherein houses of
both parties fall into this trap in recent

00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:45.930
years.

00:17:45.930 --> 00:17:50.690
So I think it's very important on the one
hand to distinguish clearly and publicly between

00:17:50.690 --> 00:17:55.790
Russia, on the one hand, and the Kremlin regime,
on the other hand, to recognize that Russia

00:17:55.790 --> 00:18:02.260
today is ruled by an unaccountable, unelected
authoritarian regime, to maintain lines of

00:18:02.260 --> 00:18:07.220
communications with Russian society and with
pro-democracy forces inside Russian society.

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:13.760
And to make it clear, and this is very important,
that a future democratic Russia will have

00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:19.190
its rightful place at the table in the future
world.

00:18:19.190 --> 00:18:20.470
That is a very important issue.

00:18:20.470 --> 00:18:25.650
But on the other hand, when we're talking
about relationship with the current regime,

00:18:25.650 --> 00:18:32.970
with Mr. Putin's regime in the Kremlin, I
think it's very important to approach them

00:18:32.970 --> 00:18:37.610
from a position of principle and from a position
of strength, because a very important thing

00:18:37.610 --> 00:18:44.790
to understand about the current regime is
that it does not consider compromise and accommodation

00:18:44.790 --> 00:18:47.310
as an invitation to reciprocate.

00:18:47.310 --> 00:18:51.580
It considers them as a sign of weakness, and
it becomes more aggressive and more brazen.

00:18:51.580 --> 00:18:57.470
And again, we already talked about how they
have become more aggressive against Western

00:18:57.470 --> 00:19:02.230
inaction and Western refusal to recognize
the reality in many cases.

00:19:02.230 --> 00:19:06.140
And I think the most important thing is to
practice what you preach and to practice the

00:19:06.140 --> 00:19:07.700
principles that you preach.

00:19:07.700 --> 00:19:10.450
And I'd like mention one thing in particular.

00:19:10.450 --> 00:19:15.540
A little more than three years ago, the U.S.
Congress passed the Sergei Magnitsky Rule

00:19:15.540 --> 00:19:20.620
of Law Accountability Act, in my view, one
of the most principled pieces of legislation

00:19:20.620 --> 00:19:23.590
ever passed in any parliament in the world.

00:19:23.590 --> 00:19:29.980
This law, as you know, is designed to end
the impunity for those Kremlin officials and

00:19:29.980 --> 00:19:34.910
Kremlin-connected oligarchs who continue to
abuse the rights of Russian citizens, and

00:19:34.910 --> 00:19:40.360
to plunder resources of Russian taxpayers
by denying them the privilege, and it is a

00:19:40.360 --> 00:19:47.920
privilege not a right, of traveling to and
keeping their assets in the United States.

00:19:47.920 --> 00:19:50.860
This is a very pro-Russian law.

00:19:50.860 --> 00:19:54.970
It's seen as a pro-Russian law by Russian
citizens, and there had been numerous opinion

00:19:54.970 --> 00:19:55.980
polls to this effect.

00:19:55.980 --> 00:19:59.630
Despite the Kremlin propaganda, the Kremlin
hasn't managed to present it as "sanctions

00:19:59.630 --> 00:20:03.650
against Russia" because the Russian people
understand that these are targeted individual

00:20:03.650 --> 00:20:08.540
personal sanctions against those who abuse
their rights and steal their money.

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:15.380
But, frankly, in the more than three years
since this law has come into effect, implementation

00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:20.160
has been very timid, and we have seen only
low-level abusers targeted so far.

00:20:20.160 --> 00:20:25.460
And I think it's important to not have any
glass ceilings with regard to this law, and

00:20:25.460 --> 00:20:34.370
to include prominent human rights abusers
as well as low-level targets.

00:20:34.370 --> 00:20:38.690
However many similarities there are between
the current regime and the Kremlin and the

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:44.070
Soviet regime, and there are many similarities
– we have political prisoners, we have media

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:48.571
censorship, we have elections without choice
as it were, and many, many other things that

00:20:48.571 --> 00:20:53.910
are similar – there is one major difference,
and that is that while they put dissenters

00:20:53.910 --> 00:21:02.050
in prison, and while they ruled through oppression
and coercion, the likes of Brezhnev, Suslov

00:21:02.050 --> 00:21:08.150
and Andropov did not keep their bank accounts
in the West, they did not buy properties and

00:21:08.150 --> 00:21:11.880
yachts in the West, they did not send their
kids to study in the West.

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:13.850
People from the current regime like to do
all that.

00:21:13.850 --> 00:21:18.661
They like to rule inside Russia in a manner
of a third world dictatorship, but they like

00:21:18.661 --> 00:21:22.070
to use all the privileges that the Western
democratic world has to offer for themselves.

00:21:22.070 --> 00:21:29.810
Now I think this double standard must end,
and the best way for a future U.S. president,

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:34.309
whoever he or she might be, to show solidarity
with those people in Russia who strive for

00:21:34.309 --> 00:21:38.380
a democratic future is to end this double
standard, and to make sure this law is implemented

00:21:38.380 --> 00:21:39.380
to its full extent.

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:42.120
Leon: Let's talk about the Russian future.

00:21:42.120 --> 00:21:50.150
Again, you're a Cambridge-trained historian
and you wear, among many hats that you wear,

00:21:50.150 --> 00:21:55.990
I've always enjoyed your analytical articles,
and I've always enjoyed your work on Russian

00:21:55.990 --> 00:22:02.150
history, and I'll mention one of them in a
moment.

00:22:02.150 --> 00:22:06.460
The future of Russia and the future of the
regime, fortunately they're not synonymous,

00:22:06.460 --> 00:22:09.700
and you're absolutely correct about this.

00:22:09.700 --> 00:22:16.260
So let's start with something that has almost
become a cliche.

00:22:16.260 --> 00:22:21.390
But like any cliche, I think it has a great
deal of truth to it, which is that there has

00:22:21.390 --> 00:22:24.840
been an implicit social contract.

00:22:24.840 --> 00:22:32.680
After the turmoil and the revolution and the
usual dislocation caused by great revolutions,

00:22:32.680 --> 00:22:40.070
the institutions that were put and planted
in the 1990s, which are described as horrible

00:22:40.070 --> 00:22:47.520
times, and times of lawlessness, and especially
corruption, that's particularly funny, the

00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:51.330
growth started and, of course, this regime
benefitted hugely.

00:22:51.330 --> 00:22:57.470
And the idea was that the people who were
first impoverished by the Soviet regime and

00:22:57.470 --> 00:23:04.450
then gone through the traditional, normal
unfortunately, dislocation on the great revolution,

00:23:04.450 --> 00:23:10.710
the regime offered a bargain, "You stay out
of politics and we see to it that your incomes

00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:15.880
grow, there's stability, and there's certain
semblance of at least personal security."

00:23:15.880 --> 00:23:25.710
And because at the beginning of Putin's rule,
oil was $18 a barrel, and then it went all

00:23:25.710 --> 00:23:31.960
the way to $140 a barrel almost steadily,
with some hiccups, pretty much that contract

00:23:31.960 --> 00:23:33.700
has been implemented.

00:23:33.700 --> 00:23:38.190
Now, this contract appears to be under threat
now.

00:23:38.190 --> 00:23:40.470
The economy contracted by 4%.

00:23:40.470 --> 00:23:47.740
It is likely to contract 1%, 2%, 3%, 4% if
the oil stays at this level.

00:23:47.740 --> 00:23:54.100
More importantly, as Putin's own advisors,
including his top advisor, Alexei Kudrin,

00:23:54.100 --> 00:23:59.100
Former Deputy Prime Minister and Minister
of Finance, said on numerous occasions, many

00:23:59.100 --> 00:24:07.160
times, Russia hid an institutional wall, the
institutions are so disfigured and so hostile

00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:13.050
to the investment climate that even if, and
that's Kudrin's opinion, even if oil is back

00:24:13.050 --> 00:24:20.080
to $100 a barrel, there's really no substantial
growth to Russia unless oil, as you put it,

00:24:20.080 --> 00:24:25.670
grows $10-$14 a barrel every year, which is
very unlikely.

00:24:25.670 --> 00:24:31.170
So let's start with this contract, between
the people and the regime.

00:24:31.170 --> 00:24:32.500
It's under threat.

00:24:32.500 --> 00:24:40.800
So could you describe what might be...and
I also should mention that the inflation,

00:24:40.800 --> 00:24:45.790
of course, is 13%-14%.

00:24:45.790 --> 00:24:51.120
Real incomes for the first time since Putin
has been in power, as you mentioned, 16 years,

00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:54.750
are down by about 9%.

00:24:54.750 --> 00:25:04.370
And of course, Putin imposed sanctions on
the imports in a country where because of

00:25:04.370 --> 00:25:14.460
the regime's economic strategy has become
essentially a...or has instituted a bargain

00:25:14.460 --> 00:25:21.290
whereby it sells oil and buys pretty everything
else with the petrol dollars, these sanctions

00:25:21.290 --> 00:25:27.580
on food and even more importantly, for example
on medicine, are hurting a lot of people.

00:25:27.580 --> 00:25:35.309
And the government is also slashing education,
they're slashing funds for medical care, social

00:25:35.309 --> 00:25:39.060
mobility is virtually stopped, we know all
those things.

00:25:39.060 --> 00:25:40.650
I don't want to go down the list.

00:25:40.650 --> 00:25:45.940
So let's first discuss what do you think the
reaction of the people will be?

00:25:45.940 --> 00:25:50.530
And also, what do you think the regime will
do to survive?

00:25:50.530 --> 00:25:53.300
Vladimir: Well, I think two things are certain.

00:25:53.300 --> 00:25:57.610
First of all, there was indeed such an implicit
social contract, as you mentioned.

00:25:57.610 --> 00:26:01.490
And secondly that it is under severe threat,
and the Kremlin is increasingly struggling

00:26:01.490 --> 00:26:06.800
to fulfill its end of that social contract.

00:26:06.800 --> 00:26:10.940
The economic situation in our country today
is indeed in free fall.

00:26:10.940 --> 00:26:13.700
Leon: Tell us what you see when you travel,
especially in provinces.

00:26:13.700 --> 00:26:16.190
I understand the situation is getting to be
quite dire.

00:26:16.190 --> 00:26:17.190
Vladimir: Sure.

00:26:17.190 --> 00:26:23.590
Well, even if you look at all the major indicators,
even at the surface, we see a double-digit

00:26:23.590 --> 00:26:28.290
inflation, we see a massive devaluation of
the national currency, more than 100% since

00:26:28.290 --> 00:26:29.290
2014.

00:26:29.290 --> 00:26:30.290
Leon: Correct.

00:26:30.290 --> 00:26:31.880
Vladimir: We're seeing a decline in the GDP.

00:26:31.880 --> 00:26:35.620
We're seeing a decline and a fall in real
incomes to the level of more than a decade

00:26:35.620 --> 00:26:37.309
ago.

00:26:37.309 --> 00:26:42.140
And sure, this is caused, to a major extent,
by falling oil prices, but here it also has

00:26:42.140 --> 00:26:47.470
to be mentioned that during his rule, Putin
has actually increased the dependence of the

00:26:47.470 --> 00:26:48.690
Russian economy on oil prices.

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:54.100
Oil and gas accounted for 40% of Russian exports
when Putin came to power.

00:26:54.100 --> 00:26:58.970
Today it accounts for 70% of Russian exports.

00:26:58.970 --> 00:27:02.480
And also it's the actions of the regime such
as the so-called counter sanctions that you

00:27:02.480 --> 00:27:07.730
mentioned also that hit Russian consumers
much harder than they did the intended recipients.

00:27:07.730 --> 00:27:11.730
But also the sanctions themselves that have
been caused by flagrant violations of international

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:14.610
law, which was also what the Kremlin regime
did.

00:27:14.610 --> 00:27:15.710
Nobody forced them to it.

00:27:15.710 --> 00:27:18.970
They chose to do it and these are the consequences.

00:27:18.970 --> 00:27:24.799
So we're seeing a major, obviously, deterioration
of the economy.

00:27:24.799 --> 00:27:30.160
And also, for example, going back to the oil
prices, today it's about $30 a barrel and

00:27:30.160 --> 00:27:33.360
we're seeing 4%-5% decline in GDP.

00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:37.220
When Mikhail Kasyanov, for instance, was Prime
Minister, the oil price was about $20-$25

00:27:37.220 --> 00:27:39.850
a barrel and we saw 7%-8% growth over the
years.

00:27:39.850 --> 00:27:44.040
So it's not, again, we can't blame everything,
or the Kremlin rather, cannot blame everything

00:27:44.040 --> 00:27:45.720
on just the oil prices.

00:27:45.720 --> 00:27:47.200
It's also the mismanagement.

00:27:47.200 --> 00:27:49.090
It's also the destruction of institutions.

00:27:49.090 --> 00:27:53.810
It's also the enormous level of corruption,
official corruption that, I think, it's safe

00:27:53.810 --> 00:27:57.230
to say is now at the highest level it's ever
been in Russian history.

00:27:57.230 --> 00:28:02.280
Leon: I apologize, Volodya, I'll let you continue,
but I just remembered something.

00:28:02.280 --> 00:28:09.020
I was on a panel with Boris Nemtsov on the
80th anniversary of Boris Yeltsin's...

00:28:09.020 --> 00:28:10.020
Vladimir: Five years ago.

00:28:10.020 --> 00:28:12.309
Leon: Five years ago.

00:28:12.309 --> 00:28:15.520
And of course, you know, there was a very
special relationship between the two.

00:28:15.520 --> 00:28:23.350
First of all, they're both Borises and, secondly,
my sense is, from what I read as I wrote my

00:28:23.350 --> 00:28:29.331
biography of Yeltsin and also from what I
heard from Boris Nemtsov, among others, Yeltsin

00:28:29.331 --> 00:28:32.730
really treated him as a son that he never
had.

00:28:32.730 --> 00:28:34.210
Vladimir: And he groomed him as a successor,
there's no doubt about it.

00:28:34.210 --> 00:28:37.320
Leon: And groomed him as a successor, that's
correct.

00:28:37.320 --> 00:28:45.929
So we were on the same panel together, and
Boris was always extremely funny and lighthearted

00:28:45.929 --> 00:28:46.929
about things.

00:28:46.929 --> 00:28:51.929
And one of the things he mentioned that I
want to say it now because of something that

00:28:51.929 --> 00:28:59.430
you said, he said, "During most of Yeltsin's
rule, the price of oil on average was $18

00:28:59.430 --> 00:29:02.490
a barrel.

00:29:02.490 --> 00:29:10.370
How many days will Vladimir Putin survive
as a ruler or as the ruler of Russia if oil

00:29:10.370 --> 00:29:12.400
were $18 a barrel?"

00:29:12.400 --> 00:29:17.320
And then he said something that was very typical
of him, he said, "He would be carried on pitchforks

00:29:17.320 --> 00:29:21.000
on the third day out of his Kremlin office."

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:29.470
I just thought I would remember Boris, and
I thought it was a fairly realistic assessment.

00:29:29.470 --> 00:29:30.470
Vladimir: Sure.

00:29:30.470 --> 00:29:36.030
And of course, Boris also was reminded that
in the summer of '98, during the financial

00:29:36.030 --> 00:29:41.700
crisis, the price of oil, I believe, was between
$6 and $9 a barrel.

00:29:41.700 --> 00:29:45.960
And again, today that government can only
dream of the level that Putin currently has

00:29:45.960 --> 00:29:47.590
–
$30.

00:29:47.590 --> 00:29:53.140
And we all the problems and all the crises
that are happening before our eyes in Russia

00:29:53.140 --> 00:29:57.210
today under the management, or mismanagement
I should say, of Putin's regime.

00:29:57.210 --> 00:30:03.040
So it's certain, I think, that the Kremlin
is increasingly struggling to fulfill its

00:30:03.040 --> 00:30:06.660
part of the social contract in economic terms.

00:30:06.660 --> 00:30:14.460
So in order to maintain its grip on power,
they're increasingly turning to repression

00:30:14.460 --> 00:30:21.560
and to a relentless propaganda, finding the
external enemies, rallying up against them,

00:30:21.560 --> 00:30:24.400
all these usual things, all these usual tricks
that are not new.

00:30:24.400 --> 00:30:25.400
Leon: Right.

00:30:25.400 --> 00:30:31.080
Vladimir: And we, as historians, we both saw
many, many examples of this in history.

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:37.140
But we also saw that these tricks and these
attempts to hold onto power usually end the

00:30:37.140 --> 00:30:41.260
same way, and I think a very good parallel
would be what happened in our own country

00:30:41.260 --> 00:30:46.960
in Russia in the late 1980s, early 1990s when,
as you remember, the political control of

00:30:46.960 --> 00:30:51.040
the communist establishment remained in place,
the propaganda machine over the television

00:30:51.040 --> 00:30:56.930
remained in place, but it simply became less
and less and less effective as the economic

00:30:56.930 --> 00:31:03.059
reality increasingly diverged from the propaganda
image.

00:31:03.059 --> 00:31:07.309
This is the proverbial battle between the
television set and the fridge.

00:31:07.309 --> 00:31:09.000
And the fridge always wins.

00:31:09.000 --> 00:31:10.720
Certainly in Russia, always wins.

00:31:10.720 --> 00:31:18.910
And I think however much the current regime
will try to cling to its power, again by finding

00:31:18.910 --> 00:31:24.590
enemies, by rallying up its propaganda machine,
by increasing repression and tightening the

00:31:24.590 --> 00:31:28.910
screws, as it were, I think the end result
will be the same.

00:31:28.910 --> 00:31:34.500
Unfortunately, and this is a very big problem,
and the Kremlin likes to accuse us, members

00:31:34.500 --> 00:31:39.720
of the Russian opposition, of being revolutionaries
and preparing for a Russian Maidan.

00:31:39.720 --> 00:31:44.850
Of course, I would say that the biggest revolutionaries
of all are the people in the Kremlin itself

00:31:44.850 --> 00:31:51.580
today, because they're the ones who destroyed
all the institutions of the state, who turned

00:31:51.580 --> 00:31:56.490
the elections into a meaningless farce, who
turned parliament into not a place for discussion,

00:31:56.490 --> 00:32:02.010
as they themselves famously said, who turned
all the major media outlets into weapons of

00:32:02.010 --> 00:32:08.620
its propaganda machine, and thus destroyed
and eliminated every single legal and constitutional

00:32:08.620 --> 00:32:12.679
way of changing the government.

00:32:12.679 --> 00:32:17.070
And in doing so, they ensured that whenever
change does come to Russia, political change,

00:32:17.070 --> 00:32:24.680
and nothing is forever, and nothing is permanent,
that this change will take the shape of some

00:32:24.680 --> 00:32:26.120
kind of a revolutionary process.

00:32:26.120 --> 00:32:29.919
As the Kremlin themselves, as Vladimir Putin
himself, will ensure that this will be the

00:32:29.919 --> 00:32:30.919
case.

00:32:30.919 --> 00:32:38.680
And I'm not saying it with any pleasure because
as a historian, as you said, which I don't

00:32:38.680 --> 00:32:45.950
think that revolutions are necessarily a force
for good, especially in our own Russian history,

00:32:45.950 --> 00:32:50.799
but this is what the Kremlin's actions are
leading to, that whenever change does come

00:32:50.799 --> 00:32:55.429
to our country, and again it will come, nothing
is forever, it will come as some kind of a

00:32:55.429 --> 00:32:56.980
revolutionary form.

00:32:56.980 --> 00:33:07.799
Leon: This is a good way, an excellent way
I should say, to lead to the next topic of

00:33:07.799 --> 00:33:12.799
our discussion, which is the future of Russia.

00:33:12.799 --> 00:33:21.750
In a broader sense, Vladimir, I think it would
not be an exaggeration to say that because

00:33:21.750 --> 00:33:30.120
of this relentless assault on what we consider
normal civilized values – human rights,

00:33:30.120 --> 00:33:39.560
human dignity, freedom of the press, honest
courts, and so and so forth – the regime

00:33:39.560 --> 00:33:46.049
has managed to create probably the most cynical
society in Russian history, the most cynical

00:33:46.049 --> 00:33:53.620
and the most corrupt, which, again as historians,
it's a hard first to take, as they would say

00:33:53.620 --> 00:33:54.620
in Cambridge.

00:33:54.620 --> 00:33:59.260
There's a great deal of competition in Russian
history.

00:33:59.260 --> 00:34:03.570
But again, I'm throwing that in to see what
you think.

00:34:03.570 --> 00:34:13.450
But as you mentioned, and again the public
opinion polls are all showing us, and I'm

00:34:13.450 --> 00:34:21.359
drawing here on a very recent annual conference
of the Levada-Center, which is perhaps the

00:34:21.359 --> 00:34:27.089
only reliable public opinion poll and independent
public opinion poll and firm in Russia.

00:34:27.089 --> 00:34:32.809
And our colleague and friend, Lev Gudkov,
who is the chairman, said that there's been

00:34:32.809 --> 00:34:40.979
the steady discreditation of the values of
democracy, human rights and lawful state.

00:34:40.979 --> 00:34:49.849
And the result was what might be called the
morally scorched Earth.

00:34:49.849 --> 00:34:57.139
In other words, the chilling picture of Russia
that we saw, for example, in this brilliant

00:34:57.139 --> 00:35:02.009
movie, "Leviathan" which was nominated and
should've gotten an Oscar for the Best Foreign

00:35:02.009 --> 00:35:14.440
Film, whereby so long as you pay to the next
level or you contribute to the next level,

00:35:14.440 --> 00:35:24.289
essentially the coterie of the procurator,
the mayor, the chief of police, and the judge

00:35:24.289 --> 00:35:28.569
could pretty much do whatever they want in
their own locale.

00:35:28.569 --> 00:35:40.980
Now, how is it that given that this has been
going on for so long, how is that you hope

00:35:40.980 --> 00:35:44.580
that you could draw on some sort of social
capital?

00:35:44.580 --> 00:35:47.910
And let's not forget the immigration of the
best and the brightest.

00:35:47.910 --> 00:35:56.080
The numbers are staggering, 200,000 in the
last few years according to the official data.

00:35:56.080 --> 00:36:02.049
And it's just the entrepreneurs, the people
who would've invented things for Russia, the

00:36:02.049 --> 00:36:07.539
people who would've opened new businesses
for Russia and created workplaces and jobs,

00:36:07.539 --> 00:36:11.400
and simply the people who would've enriched
the Russian culture.

00:36:11.400 --> 00:36:20.059
All of that has been draining hugely, not
to mention the flight of the investment capital.

00:36:20.059 --> 00:36:27.430
And yet you continue to believe that you could
draw on something to create the Russia that

00:36:27.430 --> 00:36:29.049
we talked about – free and prosperous.

00:36:29.049 --> 00:36:31.470
Could you explain why you remain hopeful?

00:36:31.470 --> 00:36:37.450
Vladimir: Well, Vladimir Bukovsky who's a
legendary figure, as you know, in the Russian

00:36:37.450 --> 00:36:38.450
human rights movement...

00:36:38.450 --> 00:36:39.450
Leon: The Soviet dissident.

00:36:39.450 --> 00:36:41.290
Vladimir: Yes, and to this day, in fact.

00:36:41.290 --> 00:36:45.140
And he once said the phrase that I always
like to remember, he said that, "One of the

00:36:45.140 --> 00:36:52.539
most surprising things about Russia is that
at any time, despite everything, whatever

00:36:52.539 --> 00:36:59.519
happens, there are always people in Russia
who are prepare to stand up to the lies, to

00:36:59.519 --> 00:37:03.309
the injustice, to the repression, and defend
the honor and the dignity."

00:37:03.309 --> 00:37:10.440
And if you look at the Soviet period, millions
of people were killed in the Gulag system,

00:37:10.440 --> 00:37:16.019
millions more were broken in the same system
of prisons and labor camps, yet millions more

00:37:16.019 --> 00:37:18.930
immigrated, left the country.

00:37:18.930 --> 00:37:24.390
And yet in the late 1980s, early 1990s, the
Russian society found to strengthen itself

00:37:24.390 --> 00:37:32.069
still to stand up and defend its newly-found
freedom and to prevent a return to totalitarianism.

00:37:32.069 --> 00:37:33.720
And I think that's a very important lesson.

00:37:33.720 --> 00:37:39.559
And it's absolutely true and it's pointless
to argue with it that Putin's rule in the

00:37:39.559 --> 00:37:44.549
last 16 years has been maintained, not just
by economic giveaways, not just by coercion,

00:37:44.549 --> 00:37:53.700
but also by a relentless propaganda that's
been fostering and emphasizing imperial complexes,

00:37:53.700 --> 00:37:58.069
mistrust, aggression, finding so-called external
enemies.

00:37:58.069 --> 00:37:59.069
Leon: And internal.

00:37:59.069 --> 00:38:00.849
Vladimir: Yes, exactly, and internal.

00:38:00.849 --> 00:38:04.320
And dividing people into us versus them, and
so on and so forth.

00:38:04.320 --> 00:38:09.150
And this will be, I think, a horrible legacy
of the Putin regime, and it'll be one of the

00:38:09.150 --> 00:38:14.150
most formidable tasks in my view of a future
post-Putin government in Russia to try to

00:38:14.150 --> 00:38:18.730
undo the damage done by this propaganda to
the fabric of Russian society.

00:38:18.730 --> 00:38:24.599
But just as always, just as before, there
are many people in Russia today, millions

00:38:24.599 --> 00:38:28.890
of them, who see a different future for our
country, a future based on the rule of law,

00:38:28.890 --> 00:38:33.140
based on political freedoms, based on the
dignity of the human being, based on European

00:38:33.140 --> 00:38:35.859
what we call modern European values.

00:38:35.859 --> 00:38:36.859
There are millions of them.

00:38:36.859 --> 00:38:41.349
I see them and I meet them as I travel around
our country, and into different regions of

00:38:41.349 --> 00:38:42.349
Russia.

00:38:42.349 --> 00:38:44.720
They are not being heard under the current
regime.

00:38:44.720 --> 00:38:45.980
They have no political representation.

00:38:45.980 --> 00:38:47.499
They have no political voice.

00:38:47.499 --> 00:38:51.039
It's designed to be that way under the present
regime.

00:38:51.039 --> 00:38:52.440
But these people are there.

00:38:52.440 --> 00:38:53.440
They exist.

00:38:53.440 --> 00:38:59.020
And in my view, they offer the best hope for
Russia's future development.

00:38:59.020 --> 00:39:08.779
Leon: Volodya, again, you touched on this
here and there, but for better or worse, the

00:39:08.779 --> 00:39:16.190
impression that we get from the U.S. media
or the people who would watch this is that

00:39:16.190 --> 00:39:21.729
it's a dictatorship, it's an authoritarian
regime, it's a brutal authoritarian regime,

00:39:21.729 --> 00:39:23.319
there is nothing that you can do.

00:39:23.319 --> 00:39:27.890
And in fact, I found that in one case, or
actually in a couple of cases, where I was

00:39:27.890 --> 00:39:34.290
writing for an elite American publications,
and I used the word "opposition," and the

00:39:34.290 --> 00:39:37.599
editor turned it into "dissidents."

00:39:37.599 --> 00:39:46.519
Because I was explained, opposition is when
there is democracy, and dissidents is in the

00:39:46.519 --> 00:39:48.349
regimes like the Soviet Union.

00:39:48.349 --> 00:39:50.890
So if you could draw a little bit, explain
that.

00:39:50.890 --> 00:39:59.180
And of course, given that, it pains me to
say it, but given that you were nearly killed

00:39:59.180 --> 00:40:09.430
last May, clearly you believe and you hope
in what you're doing.

00:40:09.430 --> 00:40:13.270
You would not be doing otherwise.

00:40:13.270 --> 00:40:17.460
So could you explain to us what is the state
of the Russian opposition?

00:40:17.460 --> 00:40:20.220
What are the venues even available to it?

00:40:20.220 --> 00:40:22.960
And secondly why are you pursuing this road?

00:40:22.960 --> 00:40:27.690
Vladimir: Well, I think it's a very important
distinction that you made between opposition

00:40:27.690 --> 00:40:34.349
and the dissident movement and, in fact, Boris
Nemtsov said not a long time before his death,

00:40:34.349 --> 00:40:38.440
before he was killed, that he no longer considers
himself an opposition leader but a dissident

00:40:38.440 --> 00:40:44.339
leader because, precisely what you said, the
opposition usually sits in Parliament.

00:40:44.339 --> 00:40:50.960
In Russia, the opposition sits in prisons,
or more often, unfortunately, in the cemeteries

00:40:50.960 --> 00:40:53.619
and that is a fact of life.

00:40:53.619 --> 00:40:59.569
But I think what keeps us going is a very
simple fact, and I can speak certainly for

00:40:59.569 --> 00:41:03.650
myself, and I'm sure for many of my friends
and colleagues.

00:41:03.650 --> 00:41:05.329
Russia is my country.

00:41:05.329 --> 00:41:06.329
I believe in it.

00:41:06.329 --> 00:41:07.720
I believe in its future.

00:41:07.720 --> 00:41:13.059
And I think the current regime is driving
it into a dead end politically, economically,

00:41:13.059 --> 00:41:17.440
in terms of the state of society, in terms
of its relations with the outside world.

00:41:17.440 --> 00:41:23.010
And I know there are millions of my compatriots,
millions of people in Russia, even today,

00:41:23.010 --> 00:41:28.019
even despite everything that's happening who
also believe in a different future for Russia.

00:41:28.019 --> 00:41:33.569
In a future based on respect for dignity and
human rights, and democracy, and the rule

00:41:33.569 --> 00:41:39.140
of law, in political freedom, in good relations
with our neighbors and the outside world at

00:41:39.140 --> 00:41:40.140
large.

00:41:40.140 --> 00:41:44.921
And these people have no political voice,
they have no political representation, but

00:41:44.921 --> 00:41:50.479
I think they offer the best hope for the future
development of Russia, and we have to do all

00:41:50.479 --> 00:41:56.160
we can to give them this political voice,
and to support them, and to present this alternative,

00:41:56.160 --> 00:41:59.349
to present this different vision for our country's
development.

00:41:59.349 --> 00:42:04.950
Sure, it's a very dangerous vocation to be
in opposition to Putin's regime, and we've

00:42:04.950 --> 00:42:07.420
seen many confirmation of this.

00:42:07.420 --> 00:42:11.279
Soviet dissidents used to have this phrase,
"The night is darkest before the dawn."

00:42:11.279 --> 00:42:18.900
And this past year has been, I think, especially
dark with the assassination of Boris, with

00:42:18.900 --> 00:42:24.140
what happened to me, the attempted murder,
and with many more jailings, and many more

00:42:24.140 --> 00:42:28.010
cases of oppression against those who disagree
with the Kremlin, and many more threats that

00:42:28.010 --> 00:42:29.410
continue to be issued.

00:42:29.410 --> 00:42:31.880
So it has been a very dark year.

00:42:31.880 --> 00:42:39.259
But I believe that the night is darkest before
the dawn, and I still believe that the values

00:42:39.259 --> 00:42:43.849
of freedom and justice will prevail in Russia.

00:42:43.849 --> 00:42:50.020
I believe that that day will come, and I believe
that it is our moral duty, and my moral duty,

00:42:50.020 --> 00:42:54.940
to do everything we can to bring that day
just a little bit closer.

00:42:54.940 --> 00:43:01.510
Leon: In practical terms, as I mentioned to
you before we started, I see your name popping

00:43:01.510 --> 00:43:09.829
up in various interviews and one of them talked
about Open Russia, the organization that you

00:43:09.829 --> 00:43:15.420
represent, extending its offers of expertise
and funding.

00:43:15.420 --> 00:43:19.809
Could you give us an insight of how do you
do it, how does that happen, and what hope

00:43:19.809 --> 00:43:21.329
there is for this?

00:43:21.329 --> 00:43:22.329
Vladimir: Sure.

00:43:22.329 --> 00:43:27.480
Well, we know perfectly well that elections
in Putin's Russia are not real elections,

00:43:27.480 --> 00:43:33.609
that they just serve as a ritual held once
every five or six years depending on Parliament

00:43:33.609 --> 00:43:37.140
or President to formalize the regime's hold
on power.

00:43:37.140 --> 00:43:42.320
The opposition is, in most cases, not allowed
to contest the elections, or in the rare cases

00:43:42.320 --> 00:43:46.000
when it is, it is suppressed and the vast
administrative resources are used against

00:43:46.000 --> 00:43:47.079
it.

00:43:47.079 --> 00:43:50.579
However, we believe that boycotts do not work.

00:43:50.579 --> 00:43:57.989
We believe that we can use even this watered-down
and sham electoral process that exists today

00:43:57.989 --> 00:44:03.380
in order to try to get our message across,
our message based on democracy and rule of

00:44:03.380 --> 00:44:06.960
law and European values to as many people
as we can.

00:44:06.960 --> 00:44:12.680
And also, as importantly, I think, to help
those opposition candidates, especially young

00:44:12.680 --> 00:44:18.200
political activists, to gain some political
experience by participating in these campaigns,

00:44:18.200 --> 00:44:21.849
to gain some name recognitions, and to gain
some reputation for themselves.

00:44:21.849 --> 00:44:28.069
And this is why we, the Open Russia Movement,
are planning to support 15 to 20 candidates

00:44:28.069 --> 00:44:31.440
for the State Duma, for the Law House of the
Russian Parliament in the upcoming Duma election,

00:44:31.440 --> 00:44:39.440
or so-called election, by offering them, as
you said, informational, legal, PR support,

00:44:39.440 --> 00:44:44.990
infrastructural support for their campaigns
by making our experts, and our specialists

00:44:44.990 --> 00:44:54.569
available for them, to help them gain this
experience and gain their name in this fight.

00:44:54.569 --> 00:45:00.069
And this is what we'll be doing, and we've
tried already, in fact, last year, in 2015,

00:45:00.069 --> 00:45:02.170
in two different cases.

00:45:02.170 --> 00:45:06.670
One was the municipal election in St. Petersburg,
and the other one was the regional election

00:45:06.670 --> 00:45:08.700
in Novosibirsk.

00:45:08.700 --> 00:45:16.519
We had in both cases a candidate whom Open
Russia was backing, and in both cases our

00:45:16.519 --> 00:45:22.619
candidate came in second, was even officially
more than 20% of the vote.

00:45:22.619 --> 00:45:26.729
And so another important goal is to show that
however much the Kremlin likes to pretend

00:45:26.729 --> 00:45:31.829
that the democratic opposition has 1% or 2%
support in Russia, this is simply not true

00:45:31.829 --> 00:45:35.130
even in the current repressive conditions.

00:45:35.130 --> 00:45:40.079
And I'm hopeful that we're going to show this
again, this time on a national level in September

00:45:40.079 --> 00:45:42.710
of this year during the State Duma elections.

00:45:42.710 --> 00:45:47.190
And, of course, there will also come a day,
and I think personally that it's not going

00:45:47.190 --> 00:45:52.480
to be that long away, when there'll be a real
election in Russia.

00:45:52.480 --> 00:45:59.249
And I think we have to start preparing for
it now and to start getting the people ready

00:45:59.249 --> 00:46:03.609
and prepared for it, and start helping people
gain this experience that they will use when

00:46:03.609 --> 00:46:06.859
the real election comes, and this is what
we're going to be doing starting this year.

00:46:06.859 --> 00:46:09.130
Leon: Thank you very much, Volodya.

00:46:09.130 --> 00:46:12.450
This is a good note to end.

00:46:12.450 --> 00:46:13.450
Thank you very much.

00:46:13.450 --> 00:46:14.450
Vladimir: Thank you very much.

00:46:14.450 --> 00:46:15.009
Happy to do this.

